But there are tricks to make it much less painful.
1.Think about both what you say and how you say it.
It’s a mentality.
You’re selling a piece of your brainchild and providing the opportunity of a lifetime to a potential investor.
It will come across if you don’t, and investors will believe you have traction.
For example, when asked, “How much are you trying to raise?” never say, “I’m trying to raise two million dollars.” You aren’t trying to do anything.
2.Don’t focus on valuation.
Venture capitalists hear pitches all day long and issue term sheets for a living–this is all they do.
The first is: “What is the valuation you are looking for?” Never answer this question unless the valuation is already set and you’re doing something like filling out a convertible note with a cap.
The market is giving us a sense of what valuation will be.”
Plus, answering this way shows you are sane on valuation, have traction, are coachable, and tactical.
Another test question is, “Who else are you talking to?” Never answer this question.
They all know each other.
Simply say, “We are talking to all of the usual suspects.
If there are people, in particular, you like to invest with, let me know who and I’d be delighted to provide them an update and work together.”
In other words, you are building a business with great technology and economics.
