Quintero explained how long ago software startups raised millions of dollars to hire teams, buy servers and develop software – but now that software is so cheap to develop, investors started funding growth rather than development.
Quintero added, “Creation has been democratized, and investors are investing in growth – not product development”.
These cost drops have resulted in an expectation by investors that the core technology has already been developed and proven before investing money.
It is beyond difficult to achieve, but hardware entrepreneurs are doing it every day.
Nearly every time I’ve spoken to Quintero since we met a few years ago, I’ve heard him warn me or others about raising too much too early.
However, this time he explained this concept to me in a way that finally clicked.
As soon as he said this, so many memories flashed through my head at once that brought it all together for me.
I also remembered a company called Skully that was raising money on Indiegogo at the exact same time as my company.
Quintero also pointed out the fact that public companies with multi-million and sometimes billion dollar budgets develop products – and most of them fail.
More specifically – how could you possibly blame failure on having too much money?
Perhaps you raised so much money that made you need to swing for the fences of winning over a mass market (to pay back your investor’s desired multiples) versus crushing it with a large niche – like Juicer for example.
Or maybe they didn’t develop anything at all, and simply delivered some beautiful product renderings.
Whenever you feel like a small fish in a big pond and the odds are stacked against you, remember that two guys in a bicycle shop beat well-funded companies to discover flight.
