Field name registrars, in case you didn’t know, are the companies that manage all the website domain names you’ll see (EG: Inc.com) – the fingerprint of your business, which may also include the hosting and privacy measures.
I’ve dug into it a little and found the few that I actually respect and would trust with what money you and I have.
It lists out all the extensions to create your domain name with prices to register, transfer, or renew for each extension, unlike some services that bury these within the website.
It will then tell you if it is available or taken.
GoDaddy used to be a place I’d recommend only as a joke but has significantly improved their service.
They still continue to offer weird things like “email services” that other hosting companies provide too.
Snap Names is a web domain name marketplace – meaning that they only deal specifically with domains that are currently owned by somebody else.
They have over three million customers, including a significant number of small businesses, which is worth it.
Finding the best web hosting service for your business can be very confusing.
Sedo is where I bought my domain – over Escrow – and is a global marketplace to buy, sell, and park domain names.
This may not be important if you’re spending $150 on a domain, but in a $15,000+ purchase – which happens in many cases – you will be potentially buying from a foreign entity, that you don’t know, and many of them will suggest an escrow payment.
This can be quite disconcerting – but Sedo is well-known and has done many, many deals – and the process is about as full of hand-holding as handing tens or hundreds of thousands of dollars for a domain name can be.
You can also purchase an existing website and a mobile app from Flippa, potentially making it a great starter for your online existence.
They also have a user-friendly control panel for buying domain names as well as offer hosting and tools for building your website.
They’re my go-to processor for names I’m just looking to hold.
They also have the classic domain name search portal and provide a tutorial on the new TLDs that helps, considering a new one seems to be appearing every second or two.
HostGator is a company I used for many years with an old firm, and I’ve found them to be fairly reliable as a rock-solid grounding for a bandwidth-heavy site.
Their prices are competitive but not the best, but I’ve found them to be immensely reliable in the past.
Host is a just-launched free web hosting company that includes domain name purchasing and registration.
Like HostGator, they include domain locking and automatic renewal, along with a user-friendly management portal (which, believe me, is pretty rare) and 24/7 technical support.
