News - The young designer subverting the ruffle to empower women
Launching a fashion brand is hard. Launching a fashion brand during a global pandemic is near impossible.
Adeola’s show in February was an opportunity to present hernewly established eponymous brandto the world. Her designs — youthful, sexy, sheer and ruffled — grabbed the attention of the fashion press and secured her “one to watch” status.
In the days after the show, the young designer was on a proverbial high, staying up for three nights before eventually crashing out.
“It was bittersweet,” said Adeola, now back in her Manhattan studio. “I was very grateful and thankful to be quarantined with my family but going from having my studio space to sharing a room with my sister … it was just a lot.”
As she tells it, her high school dissertation analyzed 16th-century Spanish dress in fine art paintings. Through her research of the works from that era, she noticed there were no Black people represented in the images, unless they were depicted as slaves or jesters.
